# Getting started with tramaj

This is a practical walkthrough. For a conceptual tour of the language, see
[Introducing tramaj](/first-article.html); for the full grammar, see the
[Reference](/reference.html).

## The fastest way in: the playground

Before installing anything, try the [playground](/playground.html) — it runs
entirely in the browser, needs no setup, and shows the same panels described
below (evaluated AST, rendered HTML, action log).

## Installing the toolchain

tramaj's PureScript packages are one [Spago](https://github.com/purescript/spago)
workspace. You need `purs` and `spago` on your `PATH`:

```bash
npm install -g purescript spago
```

Anything that bundles a browser or Node bundle — the playground and the CLI —
also needs `esbuild`, which is a devDependency of the repo root:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj.git
cd tramaj
npm install
export PATH="$PWD/node_modules/.bin:$PATH"
```

## Writing a first template

Create `hello.tramaj`:

```
@n=cardinality($ctx.items)
.p("there are `$n` item(s)")
```

and a context to evaluate it against, `hello.json`:

```json
{"items": ["a", "b", "c"]}
```

## Running it

```bash
spago run -p tramaj-cli --args "hello.tramaj hello.json"
```

This prints the evaluated `Node` AST as JSON — a `p` element wrapping the
text `there are 3 item(s)`. See [`node-json.md`](/reference-node-json.html)
for what that JSON shape means, or fold it into real HTML by rendering it
through a host like `tramaj-halogen`.

## Splitting a template into libraries

A template can `import(...)` another template as a library. Pass each one
with `--lib name=path`:

```bash
spago run -p tramaj-cli --args "--lib greeter=greeter.tramaj hello.tramaj hello.json"
```

Repeat `--lib` for as many libraries as the template's imports need.

## Inspecting a template without running it

Because imports, action keys, and context reads are all static positions
(see [Properties](/properties.html)), the CLI can answer questions about a
template without a context file at all:

```bash
node tramaj-cli/dist/tramaj-cli.js analyze imports hello.tramaj
node tramaj-cli/dist/tramaj-cli.js analyze actions hello.tramaj
node tramaj-cli/dist/tramaj-cli.js analyze holes hello.tramaj
node tramaj-cli/dist/tramaj-cli.js analyze unsupplied hello.tramaj
node tramaj-cli/dist/tramaj-cli.js analyze all hello.tramaj
```

(These need a bundled CLI first: `spago bundle -p tramaj-cli --platform node
--outfile dist/tramaj-cli.js`, run from `tramaj-cli/`.)

## Embedding in a real UI

If your host is a [Halogen](https://github.com/purescript-halogen/purescript-halogen)
app, `tramaj-halogen`'s `foldToHalogen` turns an evaluated `Node` into
`Halogen.HTML`, wiring each `action(...)` in the template to your own
`Action` type. The [playground's own source](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj/blob/main/playground/src/Playground/Main.purs)
is the reference example of a host that does this for real, interactive
clicks rather than just rendering.

If your host is something else entirely — a different UI framework, a
non-HTML document format — write your own fold over the `Node` AST; the
format is small and normative on purpose (see
[`node-json.md`](/reference-node-json.html)).

## Where to go next

- [Use cases](/use-cases.html) for where this fits alongside other
  templating approaches.
- [Reference](/reference.html) for the full grammar, builtins, and the v3/v4
  extensions.
- [For coding agents](/howto-agents.html) if you're setting an LLM loose on
  tramaj templates rather than writing them by hand.

